      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Background",
      "pageStart": 9,
      "pageEnd": 11,
      "target": "../sections/sec-000-1-background.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Background \n \nTuberculosis (TB) continues to have a significant health impact worldwide, with one third of the \nworld’s population estimated to be infected with Mycobacterium tuberculosis, resulting in so-called \nlatent TB infection (LTBI). Until recently, the tuberculin skin test (TST) was the only tool available for \nLTBI detection. The TST involves intradermal injection of purified protein derivative (PPD), a crude \nmixture of mycobacterial antigens, which stimulates a delayed type hypersensitivity response and \ncauses induration at the injection site within 48 to 72 hours.  \nThe identifica",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the background to the IGRA policy statement, noting that about a third of the world's population has latent TB infection and that the tuberculin skin test long was the only detection tool, with known limitations. It explains how IGRAs work and their growing commercial promotion in high-burden settings. It argues that their role needed evaluation in low- and middle-income countries with limited public health resources.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
      }
