      "title": "Evidence synthesis",
      "pageStart": 11,
      "pageEnd": 13,
      "target": "../sections/sec-002-21-evidence-synthesis.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Evidence synthesis  \nThe systematic, structured, evidence-based process for TB diagnostic policy generation developed by \nWHO-STB was followed:  The first step constituted systematic reviews and meta-analysis of available \ndata (published and unpublished) using standard methods appropriate for diagnostic accuracy \nstudies.  The second step involved the convening of an Expert Group to a) evaluate the strength of \nthe evidence base; b) evaluate the risks and benefits of using IGRAs in low- and middle-income \ncountries; and c) identify gaps to be addressed in future research. Based on the Expert",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Describes the evidence synthesis behind the IGRA policy, following WHO's process of systematic reviews and meta-analysis of diagnostic accuracy studies followed by an Expert Group review using the GRADE approach. It sets out how studies were selected, data extracted and quality assessed. It explains how results were pooled, or summarized when fewer than four studies were available.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
