    {
      "id": "library/10665-44759/blocks/prose-sec-013",
      "types": [
        "folio-assistant-core:Prose",
        "doco:Section"
      ],
      "kind": "prose",
      "title": "Summary of results",
      "pageStart": 18,
      "pageEnd": 19,
      "target": "../sections/sec-013-322-summary-of-results.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Summary of results \nThe majority of IGRA studies in children had been performed in high-income countries and \nextrapolation to low- and middle-income settings with high background TB infection rates was not \nappropriate.  However, based on available data, the results indicated that in low- and middle-income \ncountries: \n \n \nIGRAs and the TST had very similar accuracy for diagnosis of LTBI and active TB in children; \n \n \nMajor methodological inconsistencies between studies had a negative effect on the \ncomparability of studies and results. A key constraint was the lack of appropriate referenc",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Reports that most paediatric IGRA studies came from high-income countries, and that in lower-resource settings IGRAs and the skin test showed very similar accuracy for latent and active TB in children. It notes implementation issues such as blood collection and manufacturer support for many studies, mainly through donated kits.",
        "state": "draft",
        "draftedBy": {
          "kind": "agent",
          "id": "claude-code",
          "model": "not-disclosed"
        },
        "draftedAt": "2026-11-11"
