      "target": "../sections/sec-015-324-grade-evidence-profiles-and-final-policy-rec.md",
      "narrative": null,
      "content": "Grade evidence profiles and final policy recommendations \nThe GRADE evidence profiles are provided in Tables 3 to 6.  Based on these assessments, the Expert \nGroup concluded that the quality of evidence for use of IGRAS in children was very low and \nrecommended that these tests should not be used in low- and middle-income countries as an \nalternative to TST in paediatric TB for the diagnosis of latent TB infection, nor as an alternative to \nTST in the workup of a diagnosis of active TB disease in children, irrespective of HIV status (strong \nrecommendation).  \n \nThe Expert Group also noted tha",
      "truncated": true,
      "provenance": "ingested",
      "summary": {
        "status": "draft",
        "text": "Presents the Expert Group's finding that evidence on IGRAs in children was very low quality. It recommends against using them instead of the skin test for latent or active TB in children in low- and middle-income countries, noting harms of blood collection and unaddressed cost and acceptability.",
        "state": "draft",
